The layer-effect counterpart of #1905 (fill layers), using its `Color::in_mode`.

## Problem

In a Grayscale or CMYK document, layer style colours rendered as plain RGB: a red Color Overlay in a `Gray/8` document was pure red on the canvas and in `document.pixel`. The same went for:
- Drop Shadow, Inner Shadow, Outer Glow, Inner Glow and Satin colours;
- Stroke and glow paints;
- Gradient Overlay stops;
- Bevel & Emboss highlight and shadow colours.

An effect's colours are meant to be in the document's model: Image › Mode converts them that way (`convert_layer_colors`), and the compositors read them with `Color::to_rgb`. The commands that create or replace effects stored the RGB colour from their params, the dialog, the clipboard or a preset as is.

## Fix

- `Effect::in_mode(mode)` (doc crate): every colour of an effect in a mode's model, through `Color::in_mode`. It covers shadows, glows, strokes, overlays, satin, bevel and gradient paints; patterns are untouched. It visits the same colours `convert_layer_colors` converts on Image › Mode.
- Applied where effects are written onto a layer:
  - `layer.layerStyle.<kind>` (`set_effect`);
  - `layer.layerStyle.replace`, the Layer Style dialog's OK. The dialog's live preview runs the same command on a scratch session, so it shows what OK commits;
  - Paste Layer Style, which can paste a style copied from a document in another mode;
  - `style.presets.apply`, since preset colours are RGB.

Moving or copying effects between layers of one document needs nothing: their colours are already in its mode. RGB documents are unchanged. PSD export regenerates the effects block when the colours differ from a preserved one, as before.

## Tests

`crates/engine/src/fx_mode_tests.rs`, written first and failing on main. Each test checks every colour stored in the layer's effects (any `{mode, c, alpha}` value), so no colour is missed:

- `every_effect_command_stores_colours_in_the_document_mode`: all nine effect kinds, in Gray, CMYK and…

Closes #2789.

## What changed

PhotoCraft opens **JPEG XL** (`.jxl`) files. Read-only, like HEIC: there is no pure-Rust encoder yet (libjxl is C++), so JPEG XL joins `ASYMMETRIC_EXCEPTIONS` and Export As never offers it.

- **New crate `photocraft-jxl`** (standalone, `crates/jxl`): a thin, panic-guarded wrapper around [jxl-oxide](https://github.com/tirr-c/jxl-oxide) 0.12.6 (pure Rust, MIT OR Apache-2.0, no `unsafe` in our code). Plain data in and out (`Info`, `Decoded`, `Error`), every call under `catch_unwind`, allocations bounded by an `AllocTracker` fed from `Limits::max_alloc`. The header is parsed first so dimension limits are checked before any pixel is decoded.
- **`photocraft-codecs`**: `Format::Jxl` (extension `jxl`, MIME `image/jxl`, detected by the bare codestream signature `FF 0A` and the container's signature box), the `jxl` feature (off by default, like `heif`; official builds and CI enable it), and the adapter `codecs/jxl.rs`:
  - integer samples up to 8 bits open as U8, 9 to 32 bits as U16 scaled to the full range; 16-bit floats as F16, 32-bit as F32 with HDR values above 1 kept;
  - gray and RGB with straight alpha (associated alpha is divided out); spot colours are rendered in; CMYK files are refused as `Unsupported` rather than shown with wrong inks;
  - the header's orientation is applied once and the EXIF/XMP Orientation rewritten to 1, as for HEIC (`keep_orientation` turns the pixels back as coded);
  - the ICC profile is the embedded one, or one built from the header's colour encoding when it is not plain sRGB; EXIF comes from the `Exif` box (TIFF structure) and XMP from the `xml ` box;
  - an animation opens at its first frame with `DecodeWarning::MoreFrames`; losslessly recompressed JPEGs decode to the JPEG's pixels.
  - Without the feature, `.jxl` is still detected and opening one says "JPEG XL support isn't included in this build", never "unrecognized format".
- **`photocraft-io`** forwards the feature; File › Open and the batch commands …

## What changed

**Alpha-type channels in Multichannel PSDs.** In DisplayInfo, kind 2 is a spot ink and kinds 0/1 are alpha channels (colour indicates selected / masked areas, with an overlay colour and opacity). We imported every Multichannel channel as a black spot ink and never cleared that for kinds 0/1, so an alpha channel came back as a spot channel and was saved as one, changing the file. Now kinds 0/1 import as alpha channels with their colour, opacity and indicates. As in Photoshop, a Multichannel composite still prints them as black (transparent) ink, so the image looks the same.

**Multichannel save keeps the document's resources and global blocks.** A Multichannel save wrote only resolution, channel names, DisplayInfo and XMP, and silently dropped everything else. It now also writes:
- global light angle and altitude, and grid and guides (1032);
- saved paths, the work path and the clipping path (one path writer, shared with the layered writer);
- EXIF, with XMP and EXIF resolution kept in step with ResolutionInfo;
- every other imported resource: print settings, IPTC, the layer-id seed, and alpha identifiers while they still match the channels. Resources that only describe layers (1024, 1026, 1069, 1072) are left out;
- the document-level tagged blocks except `Layr`/`Lr16`/`Lr32`, each padded to 4 bytes as Photoshop pads them.

## Tests
- `crates/io/tests/multichannel.rs` at 8 and 16 bits: an alpha-type channel imports as alpha (masked areas, red, 50 %) and gives the same composite as a black ink. Export then reimport gives identical DisplayInfo, planes and channels. A file with IPTC, print flags, a layer-id seed, alpha ids, a target-layer resource, an `FMsk` block, guides, global light and a saved path round-trips all of them. Stale alpha ids and the layer-only resource are dropped.
- Corpus test (feature `corpus`): psd-tools `4x4_16bit_multichannel.psd` keeps its alpha channels, DisplayInfo, planes and every resource except the regenerated thumbnail (…

**TL;DR:** Camera Raw's per-pixel stages get 1.5–4× faster on a 16 MP frame, with the same pixels (at most 0.066 of a 16-bit level apart):
- Whites / Blacks 480 → 121 ms;
- HSL 176 → 52 ms;
- post-crop vignette 53 → 19 ms;
- Vibrance / Saturation 38 → 20 ms;
- white balance / Exposure 79 → 41 ms;
- Calibration 84 → 56 ms, Monochrome 60 → 39 ms.

**Stacked on #3818** (and through it #2919, #3815, #3816, #3817). The new commit is the one after #3818: [compare](https://github.com/Unemars/photocraft/compare/camera-raw-color-noise...camera-raw-fast-pointwise). #4241 (the 1:1 refinement) builds on it, and #4242 on that.

**Problem.** The develop's per-pixel stages spent their time in `powf` and in single-threaded loops:
- white balance, Calibration, Monochrome, the lens vignette and Whites / Blacks decode and encode every channel several times;
- Whites / Blacks' black and white points were summed over every pixel on one thread;
- HSL computed all eight hat weights per pixel, each through an `fmod`;
- the final clamp to 0…1 was a separate single-threaded pass.

**Change.**
- **Transfer tables** (`camera_raw::transfer`).
  - A curve is sampled at 1024 points per octave on [2⁻¹⁶, 1). The input's bits pick the segment.
  - Equal relative steps keep linear interpolation within 1.5·10⁻⁶ of the exact curve for every gamma from 0.25 to 4, near black too (a uniform table fails there on `x^(1/2.4)`).
  - Inputs outside the table (0 and below, the deep shadows, 1 and above, NaN) take the exact curve.
  - `Encoding` (the document's sRGB or power-law curve) and the sRGB curve go through it; a power-law table is built once per gamma.
- **Whites / Blacks' points** are summed in parallel bands of cell rows, in the same order as before.
- **HSL** weights come from the two bands around the hue. The hue maths avoids `fmod` where one or two exact subtractions give the same value.
- **Contrast's S-curve** is tabulated; the luminance maps for Contrast, Texture and Clarity are built in paralle…

**TL;DR:** at 100 % and above, the Camera Raw dialog no longer develops the whole frame for every edit. It keeps the whole frame's raw and tonal stages and develops the colour stages only around what's on screen. On a 16 MP Nikon D4 NEF at 1:1, against the whole develop in the same run:
- colour edits (Vibrance, HSL, Color Grading, curve, Saturation): 0.11–0.34 s instead of 5.2–7.8 s;
- Sharpening and noise reduction: 0.52 s instead of 6.5 s;
- Whites, Blacks, Contrast: 0.46–0.61 s instead of 4.7–5.6 s;
- dragging Shadows, Highlights, Clarity or Texture (the raw stage): 0.97–1.47 s instead of 4.8–6.5 s; Dehaze 2.8 s instead of 5.1 s.

The result is the whole develop's: at most one 16-bit level apart, where the colour filters' running sums start elsewhere.

**Stacked on #4240 (the per-pixel speed-up) and through it #3818.** #4242 builds on it. The new commits are the two after it: [compare](https://github.com/Unemars/photocraft/compare/camera-raw-fast-pointwise...camera-raw-visible-region).

**Problem.** Below 1:1, #3817 made the preview develop the frame at its size on screen, through kept stages. At 1:1 and above every edit still:
- read the whole area and ran the raw stage without its cache;
- wrote the result into a full-size surface and read it back;
- ran the whole pipeline over the whole frame;
- built the result as another full-size surface.

That was about 4.8 s per edit on a 16 MP NEF with typical settings, a Saturation tweak included. A new edit also waited for the running develop to finish.

**Change.**
- **Kept stages** (`camera_raw_detail_ui::develop_region`, `FullCache`, one per dialog):
  - the area's pixels;
  - the raw stage's result, quantised as Open writes it into the document, with its slider-independent `RetoneCache`;
  - the tonal stages' result.

  An edit redoes only the stages it changes. Both stages still run over the whole frame, as Open runs them (Whites / Blacks and the raw stage read the whole frame's statistics).
- **Colour stages on …

**TL;DR:** the raw stage (Camera Raw's Shadows, Highlights, Clarity, Texture and Dehaze on a raw being opened, with Exposure, white balance and colour noise reduction before them) builds its maps 2.5–3.5× faster and drags 1.5–2× faster. Every output is the same bytes. On a 16 MP Nikon D4 NEF:
- a cold retone of Exposure / white balance 500 → 142 ms; Shadows + Highlights 539 → 185 ms; Clarity 625 → 208 ms; Texture 1063 → 420 ms; Dehaze 492 → 175 ms;
- every slider at once with colour noise reduction (the first edit at 1:1) 2191 → 1281 ms;
- dragging a slider with the cache 155 → 100 ms; dragging Dehaze 991 → 481 ms;
- developing with colour noise reduction, Shadows and Clarity 1156 → 1010 ms.

**Base:** #3818 (`camera-raw-color-noise`). Independent of the other Camera Raw speed PRs (#4240, #4241, #4242): it only touches `crates/raw/src/tone.rs` and `crates/raw/src/chroma.rs`. [compare](https://github.com/Unemars/photocraft/compare/camera-raw-color-noise...raw-fast-retone).

**Problem.**
- **The cache's linear buffer.** `RetoneCache` inverted the curve for every channel of every developed pixel with a binary search. The result was concatenated from bands and then cloned, on one thread. Touching frame-sized new memory on one thread cost about 90 ms per copy.
- **The filters.** `gauss` (up to 97 taps for Texture), `box_mean` and `min_filter` read columns across memory, took a modulo for every tap and transposed their result on one thread. The fit against itself computed two box means that equal others.
- **The pixel pass** divided twice per small map per pixel to find its sample, and took the luminance's log2 once per map.
- **`set_pre`** and the luminance after Dehaze ran on one thread.

**Change.**
- **`RetoneCache`**:
  - developed pixels are 16-bit codes read back as `code / 65535`, so their inverses come from a table of all 65 536 codes; anything else is searched as before;
  - the buffer is filled in place by the workers;
  - without `pre` or colour noise reductio…

## Summary
In Photoshop, a selected live rectangle has a round widget inside each corner, and dragging one rounds the corners on the canvas. PhotoCraft only had the radius as a number (options bar, Properties panel). This adds the widgets.

With **Path Selection**, the **Rectangle** tool, or the **Move** tool with **Show Transform Controls** on, every selected shape layer that is a live rectangle:
- shows a widget at the centre of each corner arc (a few screen points inside a sharp corner, so it can still be grabbed);
- dragging any widget rounds **every corner of every selected rectangle** to the same radius (each capped at half its own shorter side);
- **Alt** changes **that corner only**, on every selected rectangle;
- the radius follows the move along the corner's diagonal from the press (dragging out past the corner makes it sharp);
- the drag previews the new outlines and commits one `shape.edit` with `radii` per changed layer, all as **one history step** (`coalesce`), so Ctrl+Z undoes it; a click changes nothing.

Feedback, as in Photoshop:
- the pointer over a widget is the arrow with a rounded-corner badge;
- a drag shows an **`R: 25.0 px`** readout beside the pointer (hidden by Preferences › Tools › Show Transformation Values, like the other readouts);
- while dragging, the transform box and the Move rollover highlight hide, and the Move tool's snapping started by the press is dropped.

Ctrl/Cmd and presses off the widgets are left to the tool. Ellipses, polygons and non-live shapes are unchanged.

